WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.734 --> 00:00:12.506
IAN SHELBACK: Hello and welcome to the Little Fox Animal Report.

2
00:00:12.507 --> 00:00:14.995
IAN SHELBACK: I am your host, Ian Shelback.

3
00:00:14.996 --> 00:00:17.593
IAN SHELBACK: This week, I am in the frozen Arctic.

4
00:00:17.594 --> 00:00:21.716
IAN SHELBACK: I am interviewing a very handsome polar bear named Chilly.

5
00:00:21.717 --> 00:00:24.243
IAN SHELBACK: Chilly has kindly agreed not to eat me.

6
00:00:24.244 --> 00:00:27.552
IAN SHELBACK: Good afternoon, Chilly. You look very handsome today.

7
00:00:27.553 --> 00:00:30.415
CHILLY: Hello, Ian. It’s nice to see you.

8
00:00:30.416 --> 00:00:37.235
IAN: People want to know what life is like for a polar bear, [n3@living so far north in this frozen country.

9
00:00:37.236 --> 00:00:41.222
CHILLY: Well, first of all, it is not a life for the weak.

10
00:00:41.223 --> 00:00:45.170
CHILLY: Many animals would not like it here. It's very cold.

11
00:00:45.171 --> 00:00:49.329
CHILLY: The Arctic is only for those of us who are hardy and strong.

12
00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:55.248
CHILLY: I am one of the strongest creatures in the world, [n3@and I am not afraid of anything or anyone.

13
00:00:55.249 --> 00:00:57.988
IAN: You do look very strong and large.

14
00:00:57.989 --> 00:01:01.212
IAN: If you don't mind me asking, how much do you weigh?

15
00:01:01.213 --> 00:01:06.929
CHILLY: Well, I weigh 720 kilograms, which is big even for a polar bear.

16
00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:14.933
CHILLY: I am 160 centimeters tall, and I am 250 centimeters [n3@from head to tail. I'm not a cub!

17
00:01:14.934 --> 00:01:20.419
IAN: No, that's for sure. You must be one of the [n3@biggest and strongest bears around.

18
00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:25.136
IAN: Tell me, Chilly, don't your feet get cold standing on the snow all day?

19
00:01:25.137 --> 00:01:26.971
CHILLY: No, not at all.

20
00:01:26.972 --> 00:01:31.895
CHILLY: My fur covers the bottom of my feet and keeps me very warm.

21
00:01:31.896 --> 00:01:36.418
CHILLY: I certainly don't need silly-looking boots like you are wearing.

22
00:01:36.419 --> 00:01:41.339
CHILLY: Look around, Ian. How many polar bears can you see?

23
00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.932
IAN: Hmm, I don't see any polar bears, Chilly.

24
00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:48.289
CHILLY: Right now there are ten polar bears watching you.

25
00:01:48.290 --> 00:01:52.203
CHILLY: But you cannot see them because they are camouflaged.

26
00:01:52.204 --> 00:01:56.185
IAN: Really? That is very good camouflage.

27
00:01:56.186 --> 00:02:00.145
CHILLY: It sure is. Hey, guys, say hello to Ian.

28
00:02:00.146 --> 00:02:03.295
BEARS: Hello, Ian. How are you today?

29
00:02:03.296 --> 00:02:04.838
IAN: Fine, thank you.

30
00:02:04.839 --> 00:02:11.719
CHILLY: Camouflage is very important for us because it helps [n3@us stay undetected when we are hunting.

31
00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:14.295
IAN: And what exactly do you hunt?

32
00:02:14.296 --> 00:02:16.727
CHILLY: Our favorite prey is seal.

33
00:02:16.728 --> 00:02:21.298
CHILLY: But seals are not easy to catch, so we have to be very good hunters.

34
00:02:21.299 --> 00:02:23.412
IAN: How do you catch them?

35
00:02:23.413 --> 00:02:26.139
CHILLY: Well, you have to be very patient.

36
00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:30.877
CHILLY: First, I look for a hole in the ice where [n3@the seals like to come to rest.

37
00:02:30.878 --> 00:02:35.787
CHILLY: Then I hide myself beside the hole and wait until a seal appears.

38
00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:40.691
CHILLY: When a seal climbs out of the hole, I grab it and eat it.

39
00:02:40.692 --> 00:02:43.262
IAN: What do you do if the seal escapes?

40
00:02:43.263 --> 00:02:47.192
CHILLY: Ha-ha! The seal almost never gets away from me.

41
00:02:47.193 --> 00:02:50.334
CHILLY: But if it does, I chase it underwater.

42
00:02:50.335 --> 00:02:55.169
IAN: If you can chase a seal underwater, you must be a pretty good swimmer.

43
00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:57.968
CHILLY: Polar bears are exceptional swimmers.

44
00:02:57.969 --> 00:03:03.484
CHILLY: Many people call us the sea bear because [n3@we can swim so far and fast.

45
00:03:03.485 --> 00:03:08.036
CHILLY: Sometimes I spend almost all day swimming in the freezing waters.

46
00:03:08.037 --> 00:03:12.815
IAN: Many animals hibernate. You know, they go to sleep during the winter.

47
00:03:12.816 --> 00:03:16.625
IAN: What about polar bears? Do you hibernate?

48
00:03:16.626 --> 00:03:21.155
CHILLY: Are you kidding? No, polar bears do not hibernate.

49
00:03:21.156 --> 00:03:24.247
CHILLY: We are too busy to waste time sleeping.

50
00:03:24.248 --> 00:03:29.447
CHILLY: We are very hard workers. But we only eat in the spring and fall.

51
00:03:29.448 --> 00:03:33.225
CHILLY: During the rest of the year, there is no food for us to eat.

52
00:03:33.226 --> 00:03:36.150
IAN: Really? How do you survive?

53
00:03:36.151 --> 00:03:43.313
CHILLY: In the spring, when the ice breaks up, we can catch plenty of [n3@seals because there are many holes in the ice.

54
00:03:43.314 --> 00:03:49.769
CHILLY: However, in the summer, when all of the ice is gone, [n3@it is too difficult to catch seals.

55
00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:54.457
CHILLY: In the fall, we can catch seals when the ice starts to freeze again.

56
00:03:54.458 --> 00:03:58.819
CHILLY: But in the winter, the ice is too thick for us to get the seals.

57
00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:04.371
CHILLY: We are able to eat an enormous amount of food at [n3@one time while the food is available,

58
00:04:04.372 --> 00:04:10.489
CHILLY: and then store our food as fat for many months for [n3@when fresh prey is not available.

59
00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:14.635
IAN: That is very interesting. What about sports?

60
00:04:14.636 --> 00:04:17.486
IAN: What do polar bears like to do for fun?

61
00:04:17.487 --> 00:04:22.998
CHILLY: Our favorite sport is wrestling. I happen to be a champion wrestler.

62
00:04:22.999 --> 00:04:27.129
IAN: I'm sure you are. How do you usually play?

63
00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:29.804
CHILLY: Let's watch!

64
00:04:29.805 --> 00:04:31.820
IAN: How do you choose a winner?

65
00:04:31.821 --> 00:04:38.937
CHILLY: When one bear gets too tired or sore, he lies down on [n3@the ground, and the other bear is the winner.

66
00:04:38.938 --> 00:04:41.112
CHILLY: I am always the winner.

67
00:04:41.113 --> 00:04:47.748
IAN: I am sure you are, Chilly. What about family? Do you have any children?

68
00:04:47.749 --> 00:04:49.739
CHILLY: I have a lot of children.

69
00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:53.540
CHILLY: In fact, my wife will be having three new cubs soon.

70
00:04:53.541 --> 00:04:55.511
IAN: Where is she now?

71
00:04:55.512 --> 00:04:57.343
CHILLY: She is in her den.

72
00:04:57.344 --> 00:05:02.517
CHILLY: When a polar bear is going to have cubs, she [n3@digs a large hole in the snow.

73
00:05:02.518 --> 00:05:07.201
CHILLY: Just before the cubs come, she goes and lives in the den.

74
00:05:07.202 --> 00:05:09.633
IAN: Do you live in the den with her?

75
00:05:09.634 --> 00:05:13.645
CHILLY: No way! I stay out in the cold all the time.

76
00:05:13.646 --> 00:05:17.562
CHILLY: Remember, polar bears are very tough animals.

77
00:05:17.563 --> 00:05:25.349
CHILLY: By the way, Ian, there is a polar bear over there that seems [n3@very hungry, and he is eyeing you hungrily.

78
00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:27.748
CHILLY: Maybe you should be going now.

79
00:05:27.749 --> 00:05:32.475
IAN: That's a good idea. Thank you very much for the interview, Chilly.

80
00:05:32.476 --> 00:05:34.900
IAN: It was a real pleasure to meet you.

81
00:05:34.901 --> 00:05:40.640
CHILLY: I'm sure it was an honor for you to meet a great [n3@polar bear like me. Good-bye, Ian.

82
00:05:40.641 --> 00:05:43.929
IAN: Let's get out of here!
